From e2fa471b5cf0d0b615d5743a4815f04609f59c70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:53:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] reaper_fw: stop feeding the DNS server's own address into
 every fqdn set

The periodic FQDN resolver parsed nslookup with an unanchored
    sed -n 's/^Address[^:]*: *//p'
but busybox nslookup_main() calls server_print() UNCONDITIONALLY before the
query (networking/nslookup.c:189), and print_host() renders every block the
same way - "%-10s %s\n" header, then "Address N: <ip> [revhost]". So the
resolver's own address arrives as

    Address 1: 127.0.0.1 localhost

BEFORE the "Name:" block, and went straight into the object's ipset. Every
*/10 pass re-added it, refreshing its timer forever, and the save block
persisted it to the /jffs cache so a cold boot replayed it back.

Three ways this was worse than it first looks:

  - A FAILED lookup poisoned the set with nothing else in it. The error goes
    to stderr (discarded by 2>/dev/null) and only the server block prints, so
    every NXDOMAIN, typo'd domain and DNS outage still added the server.
  - Where /etc/resolv.conf names the LAN IP rather than loopback (lan.c
    writes "nameserver <lan_ipaddr>" on the non-routing path), the address
    inserted into every enforcement set is the router's own.
  - The sets back both firewall rules and policy routing, so this is wrong
    data in an enforcement set, not just a cosmetic list entry.

Fix: anchor the range to the answer section, /^Name:/,$ - a failed lookup has
no Name: block and now contributes nothing. Added a leading non-routable
guard (127.*, 0.0.0.0, ::1, ::) as belt-and-braces for the same class: those
can never be a meaningful member of a FORWARD-path set whatever a future
busybox prints or a resolver answers. The permissive "let ipset do the final
parse" contract for real addresses is unchanged.

No migration needed. The fqdn sets carry timeout RFW_FQDN_TTL (3600 s) and
the design already relies on expiry to retire stale members; once nothing
re-adds the server address it ages out within the hour, and the cache is
rewritten from the live set on the next pass.

Verified without a build: reproduced the exact busybox output format for a
loopback resolver, a LAN-IP resolver and an NXDOMAIN, and ran the emitted
script unmodified against stubbed nslookup/ipset - before: 127.0.0.1 /
192.168.50.1 added, and added even on NXDOMAIN; after: only the genuine A
and AAAA answers, nothing on NXDOMAIN. Emitted text renders and `sh -n`
parses; the fprintf compiles under -Wall -Wextra -Wformat=2 with no
warnings; braces balance and the new comment introduces no premature close.
Confirmed this parse pattern exists nowhere else in the tree (ntp.c and
rwatch.c check exit status only; netool.c never parses its output).

Found by the 2026-08-28 audit (report section 0 item A / section 3 B1).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/rc/reaper_fw.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/reaper_fw.c b/release/src/router/rc/reaper_fw.c
index 619fee4518..212b0cae95 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/reaper_fw.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/reaper_fw.c
@@ -1028,10 +1028,32 @@ static void rfw_gen_resolve(const rfw_cfg_t *c)
 				any = 1;
 				/* nslookup output differs across busybox builds; take anything
 				 * that looks like a literal address and let ipset do the final
-				 * parse (same contract as Warden's feed ingestion). */
+				 * parse (same contract as Warden's feed ingestion).
+				 *
+				 * ANCHOR THE PARSE TO THE ANSWER SECTION. busybox nslookup_main
+				 * calls server_print() UNCONDITIONALLY before the query
+				 * (networking/nslookup.c:189), and print_host() renders every
+				 * block the same way - "%-10s %s\n" header, then "Address N: ".
+				 * So the resolver's OWN address arrives as a line reading
+				 * "Address 1: 127.0.0.1 localhost" BEFORE the "Name:" block, and
+				 * an unanchored match fed it straight into the set: the DNS
+				 * server was added to every fqdn object, every pass, refreshed
+				 * forever, and persisted to the /jffs cache. Worse on a failed
+				 * lookup - the error goes to stderr and only the server block
+				 * prints, so every NXDOMAIN/typo/DNS-outage poisoned the set with
+				 * nothing else. And where resolv.conf names the LAN IP rather
+				 * than loopback (lan.c writes "nameserver <lan_ipaddr>"), the
+				 * address inserted was the router's own.
+				 *   Restricting the range to "/^Name:/,$" takes answers only; a
+				 * failed lookup has no Name: block and so contributes nothing.
+				 * The leading non-routable guard is belt-and-braces for the same
+				 * class: loopback/unspecified can never be a meaningful member of
+				 * a FORWARD-path enforcement set, whatever a future busybox
+				 * prints or a resolver answers. Fixed 2026-08-28. */
 				fprintf(f,
-				  "nslookup %s 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/^Address[^:]*: *//p' | while read a _x; do\n"
+				  "nslookup %s 2>/dev/null | sed -n '/^Name:/,$s/^Address[^:]*: *//p' | while read a _x; do\n"
 				  "  case \"$a\" in\n"
+				  "    127.*|0.0.0.0|::1|::) ;;\n"
 				  "    *[!0-9.]*:*|*:*) ipset add %s%s \"$a\" -exist 2>/dev/null;;\n"
 				  "    *[!0-9.]*) ;;\n"
 				  "    *) ipset add %s%s \"$a\" -exist 2>/dev/null;;\n"
-- 
